+- **Description:** The control-char detection routine inside the `openai-chat-completions` security pre-flight block is replaced by a dedicated internal helper function that is POSIX-clean and produces correct results on BSD grep (macOS system grep without PCRE). The replacement catches all 33 control bytes -- the 32 C0 characters (0x00-0x1F) plus DEL (0x7F) -- including LF, which the prior `grep -qP` pattern missed silently. The current `2>/dev/null` suppression that caused the detection to become a no-op when grep lacks `-P` support is eliminated. Every header name and every header value is screened before any network call; any control-character match causes the script to abort with the existing die-path diagnostic naming the offending provider and (for all non-NUL bytes) the header name. NUL bytes are carved out from the header-name requirement because bash strips NUL at variable-assignment time, so the implementation must detect NUL via a file-scope pre-flight scan that runs before the awk parse -- at that point the offending header name is not extractable from the bash variable space. For NUL the diagnostic names the provider and identifies the failure class ("contains NUL bytes in header configuration") but not the specific header name. Extended test coverage pins each of the 33 control bytes as a die-path trigger and adds an explicit LF regression guard that would have caught the prior false-negative. The shell-pipeline transport path for Codex dispatch has no configurable `default_headers` surface and is therefore out of scope for this task. As a defense-in-depth measure against config.md poisoning, `api_key_env` is validated as a well-formed shell identifier (characters `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, non-empty) before resolution; the identifier is resolved via bash indirect expansion (`${!var}`) rather than `eval`. The resolved API key value is additionally screened through `_control_char_check` before being placed verbatim into the `Authorization: Bearer` header, since the same header-injection risk applies to the key as to any `default_headers` value. Dispatch order: test-writer first, implementer second (RED-verification gate between).
 - **Test expectations:**
   - Every C0 control byte (0x00 through 0x1F) supplied as a header value causes the script to exit before reaching any network dispatch call
   - Every C0 control byte supplied as a header name causes the script to exit before reaching any network dispatch call
